#{[17592186552512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552512 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40077" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552512 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552512 #edb/ref 1071 "A polynomial $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=9{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+7x-1.$ Given that $f(1)=0$, factor $f(x)$ completely." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ddb92873-9866-58c6-b7b0-d5953b026f54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552512 #edb/ref 1074 "If $f(1)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To factor $f(x)$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $9$ | $-15$ | $7$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-6$ | $1$ |\n|  | $9$ | $-6$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+7x-1}{x-1}=9{x}^{2}-6x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n9{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+7x-1=(x-1)(9{x}^{2}-6x+1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-1)(9{x}^{2}-6x+1)=(x-1)(3x-1)^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
